#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract==0.4.0",
# ]
# ///
"""deployment - project deployment standard for agent-readable runtime specs.

Agent-invoked. Run `deployment help` to reload this contract before using the
tool. This capability is provider-neutral: it owns the local deployment shape
inside a consuming project, while provider capabilities such as `coolify` own
the external substrate. A project can use deployment without enabling Coolify.

For ContextKit projects, generated Docker builds install ContextKit via the
public installer (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ai-cluster-one/context-kit/main/install.sh),
install capabilities from the lock, then initialize with the canonical sequence:
`contextkit init` (target-local bindings), `capabilities init --codex --claude`,
`capabilities doctor`, `contextkit install-hooks`, `contextkit doctor`,
`contextkit build --target all`, `contextkit audit`. Generated host bindings,
compiled context, and machine-local bindings (.env.local) are target-local build
artifacts, not deployment inputs shipped from the repo.

USAGE
  deployment setup [--profile agent-box|agent-box-checkout] [--target production]
                   [--provider coolify|dockerhost|manual]
                   [--with-telegram auto|yes|no] [--project-name NAME]
                   [--with-automations auto|yes|no]
                   [--force] [--dry-run]
      Full bootstrap for a non-technical deployment path. Creates the deployment
      declarations and the target's bundle in deployment/targets/<target>/:
      docker-compose.yaml, Dockerfile, Dockerfile.dockerignore, .env.example,
      entrypoint.sh, and (when needed) supervisord.conf. Agent-box
      setup embeds descriptor-default services from explicitly project-enabled
      capabilities in one supervised container; `enabled` remains available for
      separate Compose services. The Telegram/automations flags are
      compatibility shorthands for per-capability service policy. If --dry-run
      is passed, print the files that would be written.

  deployment scaffold
      Alias for `deployment setup`.

  deployment init [--profile generic|agent-box|agent-box-checkout|host-agents]
                  [--target production]
                  [--provider coolify|dockerhost|manual] [--force]
      Create the standard local deployment files:
        deployment/runtime.json
        deployment/targets/<target>/target.json
        deployment/capabilities.lock
      Existing files are left untouched unless --force is passed. This command
      requires an existing project `capabilities/` envelope; it never writes
      `capabilities/settings.json`.

      The profile picks the substrate `deployment sync` compiles for.
      `agent-box` builds one supervised container carrying the project.
      `agent-box-checkout` builds the same container without the project: the
      image holds tools and the boot path, and the checkout arrives from Git on
      a volume the entrypoint clones into. The profile then keeps that body
      level with the branch it tracks, in both directions, so what was pushed
      reaches the box and what the agent commits there both survives a redeploy
      and reaches the remote. `host-agents` supervises the same declared
      services as processes on the machine itself, on macOS: `sync` writes one
      launchd agent per service under `compiler.host.agents_dir`, at
      ProcessType Standard so the work a service starts gets the whole machine
      (its watchdog runs at Background), and `deployment next` hands over the
      launchctl steps. `generic` stays
      declaration-only. A Linux host uses a container profile, or `generic`
      with a supervisor the project owns. Run `deployment guide profiles` for
      the trade-offs.

  deployment freeze [--force]
      Refresh deployment/capabilities.lock from the current project capability gate.
      The file is a lightweight install list: one capability name per line,
      comments allowed. Docker builds bootstrap the capabilities manager and run
      `capabilities install <name> --allow` for each entry.

  deployment sync [--check|--adopt]
      Compile the project-enabled deployable-service descriptors into the
      runtime service graph, capability lock, and, for a container profile,
      one bundle per declared target (Compose, Dockerfile, ignore file, env
      example, entrypoint, supervisor configuration) in its folder.
      Only capabilities explicitly enabled in project settings are candidates.
      --check is read-only and exits 7 with structured drift details.
      --adopt explicitly replaces unmarked files at the runtime-declared
      managed artifact paths; external artifacts and overlays are never written.

  deployment watchdog [--dry-run]
      One pass over the launchd agents this project supervises: ask each
      service's declared doctor whether it still works, and restart the ones
      that answer no. A supervisor restarts what died; this is for what is
      alive and doing nothing, which it cannot see. Two consecutive bad
      answers are required before a restart, and a cooldown keeps a service
      that a restart does not fix from being restarted every interval.
      `deployment sync` installs this as its own launchd agent on the
      host-agents profile; run it by hand with --dry-run to see what it would
      do. Settings live under `compiler.host.watchdog` in runtime.json.

  deployment machine sync|next [--text|--json]|status|watchdog [--dry-run]
      The machine scope, on macOS: supervision for the services that run once
      per machine rather than once per project. `sync` asks the capabilities
      manager for the installed set and reads each capability's manifest
      snapshot; every one that declares `service.machine`, is allowed on this
      machine, and is not disabled in the machine declaration gets a launcher
      `capabilities-machine-<name>` and an agent
      `capabilities.machine.<name>.plist` running its `service.machine.command`
      in its expanded `service.machine.state` (home when it declares none), with
      the compiling shell's PATH, RunAtLoad, KeepAlive on unsuccessful exit,
      AbandonProcessGroup, so launchd never reaps the work the service started,
      and ProcessType Standard, so that work runs at standard priority on every
      core. It also writes `capabilities.machine.watchdog.plist`, at ProcessType
      Background, which runs
      `deployment machine watchdog`: one pass asking each machine service's
      `service.machine.doctor`, with the project watchdog's failure count,
      timeout and cooldown, and `launchctl kickstart -k` after repeated
      failures. A loaded machine agent with no pid is never kickstarted: it is
      reported as `outside_launchd` with the pid when its doctor says the
      service runs as a copy started outside launchd, as `stopped` when it
      exited cleanly (last exit code 0), since its KeepAlive restarts only a
      failure, and otherwise as `restarting`. Every other installed capability
      is listed as skipped with the reason. A machine service with no joined project is still compiled; it
      idles. The declaration is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/deployment/machine.json`,
      {"watchdog": {...}, "services": {"<name>": {"disabled": true}}}, and a
      missing file means defaults; agents and logs land in
      `$XDG_STATE_HOME/deployment/machine/launchd/`, the same from any working
      directory. `next` prints the `ln -sf` and `launchctl bootstrap` steps,
      since handing an agent to launchd stays a person's act, and a loaded agent
      takes a recompiled file only through those steps; `status` reports each
      compiled agent, whether it is linked and loaded, its compiled
      `process_type`, the `spawn_type` launchd runs it with, and
      `spawn_type_current`, false while launchd still runs an earlier
      compilation, and writes nothing. Run `deployment guide profiles` for the model.

  deployment deploy [--target <name>]
      Check compiler drift, sync the body through node-<target> over SSH,
      provision a Coolify application in the project's existing environment
      (target.environment, default production; none is created), named
      <project>-<target> after the project's ContextKit name or recorded
      slug, and set that name, its base directory and its Compose location
      (the target's bundle) on every deploy, set environment through bulk stdin, deploy and wait for healthy.
      The database is never sent: the node takes AGENTKIT_DB_* from the
      server's environment, and a deploy leaves those values standing.
      On failure request the previous release. Requires agent-box-checkout, a
      committed branch and a granted connection.
  deployment rollback [--target <name>] [--to <commit>]
      Roll back through Coolify and wait. Without --to use the previous release.
  deployment logs [--target <name>] [--build] [--follow]
      Read runtime logs or the recorded deployment build log; --follow polls.
  deployment node login claude|codex [--target <name>] [--print-command]
      SSH with a terminal into the application's agent container for subscription
      login. --print-command prepares the command without attempting login.
  deployment node sync [--target <name>]
      Fetch node-<target>, rebase and push; abort conflicts and report diverged.
      Optional target.mirror receives the synced branch. Host-agents compiles
      an interval job (target.sync_interval, default 60 seconds).
  deployment node status [--target <name>] [--json]
      JSON host, release, Git sync, harness, store and per-service placement.
      store is the node's database in force, resolved in its container through
      the shared library: level, sources and setting redacted, reachable,
      tls_enforced.
      runtime service_policy.placement maps service names to node|local|both;
      services.<name>.placement applies to custom services.

  deployment doctor [--target <name>]
      Validate deployment/runtime.json, deployment/targets/*.json, and the deployment
      references. Reports provider readiness as local facts only: whether the
      provider capability is installed/enabled here. It does not call Coolify,
      SSH, Docker, or any remote provider.

  deployment runtime
      Print deployment/runtime.json.

  deployment targets [--target <name>]
      Print target declarations from deployment/targets/.

  deployment plan [--target <name>]
      Print an execution-neutral deployment plan. The plan names the provider
      adapter that would execute the target, plus command hints, but performs no
      remote operation.

  deployment next [--target <name>] [--text|--json]
      Human next steps after setup: what to review, what secrets to enter, how
      to create the provider resource, first-login commands, and validation.

Contract commands:
  deployment help
  deployment stub
  deployment manifest --json
  deployment guide [bootstrap|standard|targets]
  deployment connections
  deployment ids list|get <label>|set <label> <value>|rm <label> [--note <text>]
  deployment refs
  deployment inventory [--network]

FILES
  deployment/runtime.json
      The project's runtime declaration: profile, services, service-level
      required env, state volumes, runtime capability exclusions, capability
      lock path, and service_policy and compiler layout. A container runtime
      that names no artifact path compiles one bundle per target (below).
      compiler.artifacts declares the
      Dockerfile, entrypoint, env example, dockerignore, and optional supervisor paths;
      compose_file declares the generated base Compose file; either one keeps
      the runtime on those declared paths instead of per-target bundles;
      compiler.compose_overlays declares untouched project-owned overlays;
      compiler.container supplies agent_home and project_root for portable mounts;
      compiler.build_args is the only source of generated Compose build args.
      A service environment_defaults mapping overrides descriptor env defaults.

  Runtime-declared compiler artifact paths
      A string path is compiler-managed for compatibility. Object declarations
      use {path, ownership}, where ownership is managed or external. External
      files must exist but are excluded from drift, sync, and adoption. Setup
      declares none, so its runtime compiles per-target bundles; declared local
      Compose overlays are external.

  deployment/targets/<target>/
      One folder per deployment target. target.json chooses a provider
      (`coolify`, `dockerhost`, or `manual`) and carries only the adapter-facing
      resource handle needed to execute that target; a declaration still at
      deployment/targets/<target>.json is read until it is moved. Beside it,
      sync compiles the target's bundle: docker-compose.yaml (build context
      ../../.., the project root), Dockerfile, Dockerfile.dockerignore (Docker
      reads it for that Dockerfile), .env.example, entrypoint.sh,
      supervisord.conf when Supervisor runs, and body-sync.sh on the checkout
      profile. The project root gains only .gitattributes on that profile.

  deployment/capabilities.lock
      Lightweight install list for the agent image: one capability per line.
      It is a deployment input, not a capability manager file. The runtime's
      capabilities.exclude list removes operator-only tools from the image
      without changing their project policy on the host.

  capabilities/deployment/reference/*.md
      Optional project-specific operational models. Reference files are neither
      created nor required by deployment init or setup. When present, they hold
      genuine project-specific deployment context rather than pointers
      duplicating runtime.json or target JSON schemas. Loaded on demand by
      `deployment refs` and the manager's generated capability context.

EXIT CODES
  0 success
  3 not found (no project, no runtime, unknown target)
  4 project policy refused this capability
  6 malformed input, validation failure, or init conflict
  7 deployment sync --check drift
"""

